('node-4-26', 'phase-04-lesson-26-tcp-reliability-flow-control-congestion-c', 'module-6', 26, 'Lesson 6.26: TCP Reliability, Flow Control, & Congestion Control', 'Prerequisites: Lesson 4.25', 'Prerequisites: Lesson 4.25 | Subtopics: 4 items', 'Systems project application', 180, 5, 1120.0, 1300.0, '# Lesson 4.26: TCP Reliability, Flow Control, & Congestion Control

- **Status**: `[State: Active | Complete Specification | Core]`
- **Prerequisites**: Lesson 4.25
- **Subtopics**:
  - `4.26.1` Sequence numbers and cumulative ACKs: detecting packet loss, duplicates, and out-of-order delivery.
  - `4.26.2` Sliding Window Flow Control: receiver''s Advertised Window (`rwnd`) preventing sender from overwhelming receiver''s buffer.
  - `4.26.3` Zero Window Probing: sender probing receiver when window drops to 0.
  - `4.26.4` Congestion Control: Congestion Window (`cwnd`), Slow Start, Congestion Avoidance, Fast Retransmit (3 duplicate ACKs), Fast Recovery; TCP CUBIC and BBR.
- **Key Failure Modes & Edge Cases**: Packet buffer bloat causing severe latency spikes on lossy network connections.
- **Verification & Mastery Check**: Simulate packet loss using Linux `tc` (traffic control) and observe TCP window contraction and retransmission behavior.
- **Project Application**: Systems network optimization.', '{"solution.py": "# Phase 4 // Lesson 4.26: TCP Reliability, Flow Control, & Congestion Control\n\ndef solve():\n    \"\"\"\n    Verification: Simulate packet loss using Linux `tc` (traffic control) and observe TCP window contraction and retra\n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n"}'::jsonb, '{"lesson": "4.26", "subtopics_count": 4, "verification_criteria": "Simulate packet loss using Linux `tc` (traffic control) and observe TCP window contraction and retransmission behavior.", "subtopics": ["4.26.1 Sequence numbers and cumulative ACKs: detecting packet loss, duplicates, and out-of-order delivery.", "4.26.2 Sliding Window Flow Control: receiver''s Advertised Window (`rwnd`) preventing sender from overwhelming receiver''s buffer.", "4.26.3 Zero Window Probing: sender probing receiver when window drops to 0.", "4.26.4 Congestion Control: Congestion Window (`cwnd`), Slow Start, Congestion Avoidance, Fast Retransmit (3 duplicate ACKs), Fast Recovery; TCP CUBIC and BBR."]}'::jsonb, '["Explain how this implementation prevents: Packet buffer bloat causing severe latency spikes on lossy network connections.", "How does TCP Reliability, Flow Control, & Congestion Control scale under memory and concurrency constraints?", "Defend the architectural tradeoffs of this design in production."]'::jsonb),
('node-4-27', 'phase-04-lesson-27-dns-resolution-record-types-caching-hiera', 'module-6', 27, 'Lesson 6.27: DNS Resolution, Record Types, & Caching Hierarchies', 'Prerequisites: Lesson 4.25', 'Prerequisites: Lesson 4.25 | Subtopics: 4 items', 'Systems project application', 180, 5, 1000.0, 1350.0, '# Lesson 4.27: DNS Resolution, Record Types, & Caching Hierarchies

- **Status**: `[State: Active | Complete Specification | Core]`
- **Prerequisites**: Lesson 4.25
- **Subtopics**:
  - `4.27.1` DNS hierarchy: Root servers (13 named authorities), Top-Level Domain (TLD) servers, Authoritative nameservers.
  - `4.27.2` Recursive Resolvers vs Iterative Resolvers; caching and Time-To-Live (TTL) expiration.
  - `4.27.3` Record types: `A` (IPv4), `AAAA` (IPv6), `CNAME` (canonical alias), `MX` (mail), `TXT` (SPF/verification), `SRV` (service discovery).
  - `4.27.4` Debugging DNS with `dig`: `dig +trace`, `dig +short`, inspecting EDNS client subnet headers.
- **Key Failure Modes & Edge Cases**: DNS TTL misconfigurations causing multi-day customer outages after cloud IP address migrations.
- **Verification & Mastery Check**: Perform a full recursive trace of a domain from root servers to authoritative nameservers using `dig +trace`.
- **Project Application**: InfraBlueprint: Cloud DNS configuration in Phase 7.', '{"solution.py": "# Phase 4 // Lesson 4.27: DNS Resolution, Record Types, & Caching Hierarchies\n\ndef solve():\n    \"\"\"\n    Verification: Perform a full recursive trace of a domain from root servers to authoritative nameservers using `dig\n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n"}'::jsonb, '{"lesson": "4.27", "subtopics_count": 4, "verification_criteria": "Perform a full recursive trace of a domain from root servers to authoritative nameservers using `dig +trace`.", "subtopics": ["4.27.1 DNS hierarchy: Root servers (13 named authorities), Top-Level Domain (TLD) servers, Authoritative nameservers.", "4.27.2 Recursive Resolvers vs Iterative Resolvers; caching and Time-To-Live (TTL) expiration.", "4.27.3 Record types: `A` (IPv4), `AAAA` (IPv6), `CNAME` (canonical alias), `MX` (mail), `TXT` (SPF/verification), `SRV` (service discovery).", "4.27.4 Debugging DNS with `dig`: `dig +trace`, `dig +short`, inspecting EDNS client subnet headers."]}'::jsonb, '["Explain how this implementation prevents: DNS TTL misconfigurations causing multi-day customer outages after cloud IP address migrations.", "How does DNS Resolution, Record Types, & Caching Hierarchies scale under memory and concurrency constraints?", "Defend the architectural tradeoffs of this design in production."]'::jsonb),
('node-4-28', 'phase-04-lesson-28-tls-1-3-handshake-perfect-forward-secrecy', 'module-6', 28, 'Lesson 6.28: TLS 1.3 Handshake, Perfect Forward Secrecy, & PKI', 'Prerequisites: Lesson 4.25', 'Prerequisites: Lesson 4.25 | Subtopics: 4 items', 'Systems project application', 180, 5, 1060.0, 1400.0, '# Lesson 4.28: TLS 1.3 Handshake, Perfect Forward Secrecy, & PKI

- **Status**: `[State: Active | Complete Specification | Core]`
- **Prerequisites**: Lesson 4.25
- **Subtopics**:
  - `4.28.1` Transport Layer Security (TLS 1.3) vs legacy TLS 1.2: removing insecure ciphers, mandating 1-RTT handshake.
  - `4.28.2` Key Exchange via Ephemeral Diffie-Hellman (ECDHE): deriving session keys over insecure channels without transmitting secrets.
  - `4.28.3` Perfect Forward Secrecy (PFS): compromising server private key does NOT compromise previously recorded session traffic.
  - `4.28.4` Public Key Infrastructure (PKI): X.509 certificate format, Certificate Authorities, intermediate chains, OCSP stapling.
- **Key Failure Modes & Edge Cases**: Serving broken certificate chains lacking intermediate CA certificates, causing untrusted certificate warnings on mobile clients.
- **Verification & Mastery Check**: Inspect a TLS 1.3 handshake using `openssl s_client -connect host:443 -tls1_3` and verify cipher suite and certificate chain.
- **Project Application**: InfraBlueprint: SSL termination.', '{"solution.py": "# Phase 4 // Lesson 4.28: TLS 1.3 Handshake, Perfect Forward Secrecy, & PKI\n\ndef solve():\n    \"\"\"\n    Verification: Inspect a TLS 1.3 handshake using `openssl s_client -connect host:443 -tls1_3` and verify cipher sui\n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n"}'::jsonb, '{"lesson": "4.28", "subtopics_count": 4, "verification_criteria": "Inspect a TLS 1.3 handshake using `openssl s_client -connect host:443 -tls1_3` and verify cipher suite and certificate chain.", "subtopics": ["4.28.1 Transport Layer Security (TLS 1.3) vs legacy TLS 1.2: removing insecure ciphers, mandating 1-RTT handshake.", "4.28.2 Key Exchange via Ephemeral Diffie-Hellman (ECDHE): deriving session keys over insecure channels without transmitting secrets.", "4.28.3 Perfect Forward Secrecy (PFS): compromising server private key does NOT compromise previously recorded session traffic.", "4.28.4 Public Key Infrastructure (PKI): X.509 certificate format, Certificate Authorities, intermediate chains, OCSP stapling."]}'::jsonb, '["Explain how this implementation prevents: Serving broken certificate chains lacking intermediate CA certificates, causing untrusted certificate warnings on mobile", "How does TLS 1.3 Handshake, Perfect Forward Secrecy, & PKI scale under memory and concurrency constraints?", "Defend the architectural tradeoffs of this design in production."]'::jsonb),
('node-4-29', 'phase-04-lesson-29-http-1-1-vs-http-2-vs-http-3-quic-framing', 'module-6', 29, 'Lesson 6.29: HTTP/1.1 vs HTTP/2 vs HTTP/3 (QUIC) Framing', 'Prerequisites: Lesson 4.25', 'Prerequisites: Lesson 4.25 | Subtopics: 4 items', 'Systems project application', 180, 5, 1120.0, 1450.0, '# Lesson 4.29: HTTP/1.1 vs HTTP/2 vs HTTP/3 (QUIC) Framing

- **Status**: `[State: Active | Complete Specification | Core]`
- **Prerequisites**: Lesson 4.25
- **Subtopics**:
  - `4.29.1` HTTP/1.1 limitations: plaintext framing, Head-of-Line (HoL) blocking on TCP connection, persistent connection reuse.
  - `4.29.2` HTTP/2 binary framing: Streams, Frames (HEADERS, DATA, SETTINGS), stream multiplexing over a single TCP connection, HPACK compression.
  - `4.29.3` HTTP/2 Head-of-Line blocking at TCP level: a single dropped packet stalls all multiplexed streams.
  - `4.29.4` HTTP/3 & QUIC: running over UDP; independent streams eliminating TCP HoL blocking; 0-RTT connection resumption; connection migration across IP changes.
- **Key Failure Modes & Edge Cases**: Creating dozens of parallel TCP connections for HTTP/1.1 domain sharding when HTTP/2 multiplexing makes it an anti-pattern.
- **Verification & Mastery Check**: Capture and decode HTTP/2 binary frames using Wireshark, identifying stream IDs and HPACK header compression.
- **Project Application**: NanoHTTP and API gateway architectures.', '{"solution.py": "# Phase 4 // Lesson 4.29: HTTP/1.1 vs HTTP/2 vs HTTP/3 (QUIC) Framing\n\ndef solve():\n    \"\"\"\n    Verification: Capture and decode HTTP/2 binary frames using Wireshark, identifying stream IDs and HPACK header com\n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n"}'::jsonb, '{"lesson": "4.29", "subtopics_count": 4, "verification_criteria": "Capture and decode HTTP/2 binary frames using Wireshark, identifying stream IDs and HPACK header compression.", "subtopics": ["4.29.1 HTTP/1.1 limitations: plaintext framing, Head-of-Line (HoL) blocking on TCP connection, persistent connection reuse.", "4.29.2 HTTP/2 binary framing: Streams, Frames (HEADERS, DATA, SETTINGS), stream multiplexing over a single TCP connection, HPACK compression.", "4.29.3 HTTP/2 Head-of-Line blocking at TCP level: a single dropped packet stalls all multiplexed streams.", "4.29.4 HTTP/3 & QUIC: running over UDP; independent streams eliminating TCP HoL blocking; 0-RTT connection resumption; connection migration across IP changes."]}'::jsonb, '["Explain how this implementation prevents: Creating dozens of parallel TCP connections for HTTP/1.1 domain sharding when HTTP/2 multiplexing makes it an anti-patte", "How does HTTP/1.1 vs HTTP/2 vs HTTP/3 (QUIC) Framing scale under memory and concurrency constraints?", "Defend the architectural tradeoffs of this design in production."]'::jsonb),
('node-4-30', 'phase-04-lesson-30-network-socket-benchmarking-with-wrk', 'module-6', 30, 'Lesson 6.30: Network & Socket Benchmarking with `wrk`', 'Prerequisites: Lesson 4.25', 'Prerequisites: Lesson 4.25 | Subtopics: 4 items', 'Systems project application', 180, 5, 1000.0, 1500.0, '# Lesson 4.30: Network & Socket Benchmarking with `wrk`

- **Status**: `[State: Active | Complete Specification | Core]`
- **Prerequisites**: Lesson 4.25
- **Subtopics**:
  - `4.30.1` Benchmarking methodology: open vs closed workload models; Coordinated Omission problem and latency percentile skew.
  - `4.30.2` The `wrk` benchmarking engine: multi-threaded, epoll-driven load generation; executing Lua scripts for dynamic payloads.
  - `4.30.3` Interpreting metrics: Throughput (RPS), Latency percentiles (p50, p90, p99, p99.9), Error counts.
  - `4.30.4` OS kernel tuning for high load: `sysctl` parameters (`net.core.somaxconn`, `net.ipv4.tcp_max_syn_backlog`, ephemeral port ranges).
- **Key Failure Modes & Edge Cases**: Reporting average latency instead of p99/p99.9 tail latencies, hiding severe multi-second stutter affecting 1% of users.
- **Verification & Mastery Check**: Execute a load test with `wrk -t4 -c100 -d30s` against an HTTP server, measure p99 latency, and identify kernel bottlenecks.
- **Project Application**: NanoHTTP: Performance benchmarking.', '{"solution.py": "# Phase 4 // Lesson 4.30: Network & Socket Benchmarking with `wrk`\n\ndef solve():\n    \"\"\"\n    Verification: Execute a load test with `wrk -t4 -c100 -d30s` against an HTTP server, measure p99 latency, and iden\n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n"}'::jsonb, '{"lesson": "4.30", "subtopics_count": 4, "verification_criteria": "Execute a load test with `wrk -t4 -c100 -d30s` against an HTTP server, measure p99 latency, and identify kernel bottlenecks.", "subtopics": ["4.30.1 Benchmarking methodology: open vs closed workload models; Coordinated Omission problem and latency percentile skew.", "4.30.2 The `wrk` benchmarking engine: multi-threaded, epoll-driven load generation; executing Lua scripts for dynamic payloads.", "4.30.3 Interpreting metrics: Throughput (RPS), Latency percentiles (p50, p90, p99, p99.9), Error counts.", "4.30.4 OS kernel tuning for high load: `sysctl` parameters (`net.core.somaxconn`, `net.ipv4.tcp_max_syn_backlog`, ephemeral port ranges)."]}'::jsonb, '["Explain how this implementation prevents: Reporting average latency instead of p99/p99.9 tail latencies, hiding severe multi-second stutter affecting 1% of users.", "How does Network & Socket Benchmarking with `wrk` scale under memory and concurrency constraints?", "Defend the architectural tradeoffs of this design in production."]'::jsonb),
('node-4-31', 'phase-04-lesson-31-operating-system-metrics-cpu-load-average', 'module-6', 31, 'Lesson 6.31: Operating System Metrics: CPU, Load Average, & Runqueues', 'Prerequisites: Phase 0 (Lesson 0.5)', 'Prerequisites: Phase 0 (Lesson 0.5) | Subtopics: 4 items', 'Systems project application', 180, 5, 1060.0, 1550.0, '# Lesson 4.31: Operating System Metrics: CPU, Load Average, & Runqueues

- **Status**: `[State: Active | Complete Specification | Core]`
- **Prerequisites**: Phase 0 (Lesson 0.5)
- **Subtopics**:
  - `4.31.1` CPU metrics: User time (%usr), System time (%sys), I/O wait (%iowait), Idle time (%idle), Steal time (%steal).
  - `4.31.2` Understanding Linux Load Average: 1, 5, 15-minute metrics; count of processes in TASK_RUNNING and TASK_UNINTERRUPTIBLE states.
  - `4.31.3` CPU Runqueues: measuring thread scheduling queues via `vmstat` and identifying CPU saturation.
  - `4.31.4` Distinguishing CPU saturation from I/O bottleneck using `vmstat` and `iostat`.
- **Key Failure Modes & Edge Cases**: Misinterpreting high `%iowait` as a CPU processing bottleneck when the disk storage array is saturated.
- **Verification & Mastery Check**: Run a CPU stress script, observe load average and runqueue depth using `vmstat 1`, and analyze metric changes.
- **Project Application**: SysTrace: System health dashboard.', '{"solution.py": "# Phase 4 // Lesson 4.31: Operating System Metrics: CPU, Load Average, & Runqueues\n\ndef solve():\n    \"\"\"\n    Verification: Run a CPU stress script, observe load average and runqueue depth using `vmstat 1`, and analyze metri\n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n"}'::jsonb, '{"lesson": "4.31", "subtopics_count": 4, "verification_criteria": "Run a CPU stress script, observe load average and runqueue depth using `vmstat 1`, and analyze metric changes.", "subtopics": ["4.31.1 CPU metrics: User time (%usr), System time (%sys), I/O wait (%iowait), Idle time (%idle), Steal time (%steal).", "4.31.2 Understanding Linux Load Average: 1, 5, 15-minute metrics; count of processes in TASK_RUNNING and TASK_UNINTERRUPTIBLE states.", "4.31.3 CPU Runqueues: measuring thread scheduling queues via `vmstat` and identifying CPU saturation.", "4.31.4 Distinguishing CPU saturation from I/O bottleneck using `vmstat` and `iostat`."]}'::jsonb, '["Explain how this implementation prevents: Misinterpreting high `%iowait` as a CPU processing bottleneck when the disk storage array is saturated.", "How does Operating System Metrics: CPU, Load Average, & Runqueues scale under memory and concurrency constraints?", "Defend the architectural tradeoffs of this design in production."]'::jsonb),
('node-4-32', 'phase-04-lesson-32-disk-i-o-profiling-iops-latency-iostat', 'module-6', 32, 'Lesson 6.32: Disk I/O Profiling: IOPS, Latency, & `iostat`', 'Prerequisites: Lesson 4.31', 'Prerequisites: Lesson 4.31 | Subtopics: 4 items', 'Systems project application', 180, 5, 1120.0, 1600.0, '# Lesson 4.32: Disk I/O Profiling: IOPS, Latency, & `iostat`

- **Status**: `[State: Active | Complete Specification | Core]`
- **Prerequisites**: Lesson 4.31
- **Subtopics**:
  - `4.32.1` Disk performance primitives: Throughput (MB/s) vs Input/Output Operations Per Second (IOPS).
  - `4.32.2` Rotational latency and seek time (HDD) vs NAND flash memory cell wear and page read/write (SSD/NVMe).
  - `4.32.3` Using `iostat -xz 1`: r/s (read IOPS), w/s (write IOPS), r_await / w_await (I/O latency in ms), %util (device saturation).
  - `4.32.4` Write Amplification in SSDs and file system page cache flushing (`sync`, `fsync`).
- **Key Failure Modes & Edge Cases**: Saturating disk IOPS with unbuffered random small writes, causing global application latency spikes.
- **Verification & Mastery Check**: Generate random disk write workloads using `dd` and profile IOPS, wait times, and utilization using `iostat`.
- **Project Application**: Database performance tuning in Phase 5.', '{"solution.py": "# Phase 4 // Lesson 4.32: Disk I/O Profiling: IOPS, Latency, & `iostat`\n\ndef solve():\n    \"\"\"\n    Verification: Generate random disk write workloads using `dd` and profile IOPS, wait times, and utilization using \n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n"}'::jsonb, '{"lesson": "4.32", "subtopics_count": 4, "verification_criteria": "Generate random disk write workloads using `dd` and profile IOPS, wait times, and utilization using `iostat`.", "subtopics": ["4.32.1 Disk performance primitives: Throughput (MB/s) vs Input/Output Operations Per Second (IOPS).", "4.32.2 Rotational latency and seek time (HDD) vs NAND flash memory cell wear and page read/write (SSD/NVMe).", "4.32.3 Using `iostat -xz 1`: r/s (read IOPS), w/s (write IOPS), r_await / w_await (I/O latency in ms), %util (device saturation).", "4.32.4 Write Amplification in SSDs and file system page cache flushing (`sync`, `fsync`)."]}'::jsonb, '["Explain how this implementation prevents: Saturating disk IOPS with unbuffered random small writes, causing global application latency spikes.", "How does Disk I/O Profiling: IOPS, Latency, & `iostat` scale under memory and concurrency constraints?", "Defend the architectural tradeoffs of this design in production."]'::jsonb),
('node-4-33', 'phase-04-lesson-33-network-profiling-sockets-drops-ss-netsta', 'module-6', 33, 'Lesson 6.33: Network Profiling: Sockets, Drops, & `ss` / `netstat`', 'Prerequisites: Lesson 4.25', 'Prerequisites: Lesson 4.25 | Subtopics: 4 items', 'Systems project application', 180, 5, 1000.0, 1650.0, '# Lesson 4.33: Network Profiling: Sockets, Drops, & `ss` / `netstat`

- **Status**: `[State: Active | Complete Specification | Core]`
- **Prerequisites**: Lesson 4.25
- **Subtopics**:
  - `4.33.1` Socket statistics with `ss`: inspecting TCP socket states (`ss -tulpn`), send/receive buffer queues.
  - `4.33.2` Detecting dropped packets: parsing `/proc/net/snmp` and `netstat -s` (ListenOverflows, ListenDrops).
  - `4.33.3` Socket buffer queue filling: identifying slow application processing when Recv-Q remains non-zero.
  - `4.33.4` Bandwidth monitoring with `iftop` and `nload`; packet loss detection with `mtr`.
- **Key Failure Modes & Edge Cases**: Ignoring `ListenOverflows` in `ss -s`, missing silent kernel TCP connection drops during traffic bursts.
- **Verification & Mastery Check**: Simulate socket backlog saturation on an HTTP server and observe `ListenDrops` counters incrementing in real time.
- **Project Application**: SysTrace and InfraBlueprint.', '{"solution.py": "# Phase 4 // Lesson 4.33: Network Profiling: Sockets, Drops, & `ss` / `netstat`\n\ndef solve():\n    \"\"\"\n    Verification: Simulate socket backlog saturation on an HTTP server and observe `ListenDrops` counters incrementing\n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n"}'::jsonb, '{"lesson": "4.33", "subtopics_count": 4, "verification_criteria": "Simulate socket backlog saturation on an HTTP server and observe `ListenDrops` counters incrementing in real time.", "subtopics": ["4.33.1 Socket statistics with `ss`: inspecting TCP socket states (`ss -tulpn`), send/receive buffer queues.", "4.33.2 Detecting dropped packets: parsing `/proc/net/snmp` and `netstat -s` (ListenOverflows, ListenDrops).", "4.33.3 Socket buffer queue filling: identifying slow application processing when Recv-Q remains non-zero.", "4.33.4 Bandwidth monitoring with `iftop` and `nload`; packet loss detection with `mtr`."]}'::jsonb, '["Explain how this implementation prevents: Ignoring `ListenOverflows` in `ss -s`, missing silent kernel TCP connection drops during traffic bursts.", "How does Network Profiling: Sockets, Drops, & `ss` / `netstat` scale under memory and concurrency constraints?", "Defend the architectural tradeoffs of this design in production."]'::jsonb),
('node-4-34', 'phase-04-lesson-34-dynamic-tracing-with-strace-kernel-call-p', 'module-6', 34, 'Lesson 6.34: Dynamic Tracing with `strace` & Kernel Call Profiling', 'Prerequisites: Phase 0 (Lesson 0.17)', 'Prerequisites: Phase 0 (Lesson 0.17) | Subtopics: 4 items', 'Systems project application', 180, 5, 1060.0, 1700.0, '# Lesson 4.34: Dynamic Tracing with `strace` & Kernel Call Profiling

- **Status**: `[State: Active | Complete Specification | Core]`
- **Prerequisites**: Phase 0 (Lesson 0.17)
- **Subtopics**:
  - `4.34.1` Attaching `strace` to running processes (`strace -p <PID> -f`): following child forks and threads.
  - `4.34.2` Syscall timing analysis: `strace -T` measuring elapsed time spent inside individual kernel system calls.
  - `4.34.3` Syscall aggregation: `strace -c` producing summary tables of calls, errors, and percentage time spent.
  - `4.34.4` The performance overhead of ptrace: why `strace` slows target processes by 10x and must be used with caution in production.
- **Key Failure Modes & Edge Cases**: Running `strace` on high-traffic production databases, causing severe performance degradation due to ptrace breakpoint traps.
- **Verification & Mastery Check**: Diagnose an unfamiliar hanging process using `strace -p <PID>` and identify the blocking system call.
- **Project Application**: SysTrace: Core debugging foundation.', '{"solution.py": "# Phase 4 // Lesson 4.34: Dynamic Tracing with `strace` & Kernel Call Profiling\n\ndef solve():\n    \"\"\"\n    Verification: Diagnose an unfamiliar hanging process using `strace -p <PID>` and identify the blocking system call\n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n"}'::jsonb, '{"lesson": "4.34", "subtopics_count": 4, "verification_criteria": "Diagnose an unfamiliar hanging process using `strace -p <PID>` and identify the blocking system call.", "subtopics": ["4.34.1 Attaching `strace` to running processes (`strace -p <PID> -f`): following child forks and threads.", "4.34.2 Syscall timing analysis: `strace -T` measuring elapsed time spent inside individual kernel system calls.", "4.34.3 Syscall aggregation: `strace -c` producing summary tables of calls, errors, and percentage time spent.", "4.34.4 The performance overhead of ptrace: why `strace` slows target processes by 10x and must be used with caution in production."]}'::jsonb, '["Explain how this implementation prevents: Running `strace` on high-traffic production databases, causing severe performance degradation due to ptrace breakpoint t", "How does Dynamic Tracing with `strace` & Kernel Call Profiling scale under memory and concurrency constraints?", "Defend the architectural tradeoffs of this design in production."]'::jsonb),
('node-4-35', 'phase-04-lesson-35-linux-namespaces-process-mount-uts-isolat', 'module-6', 35, 'Lesson 6.35: Linux Namespaces: Process, Mount, & UTS Isolation', 'Prerequisites: Phase 0 (Lesson 0.2)', 'Prerequisites: Phase 0 (Lesson 0.2) | Subtopics: 4 items', 'Systems project application', 180, 5, 1120.0, 1750.0, '# Lesson 4.35: Linux Namespaces: Process, Mount, & UTS Isolation

- **Status**: `[State: Active | Complete Specification | Core]`
- **Prerequisites**: Phase 0 (Lesson 0.2)
- **Subtopics**:
  - `4.35.1` Demystifying containers: containers are standard Linux processes isolated via kernel namespaces and cgroups.
  - `4.35.2` PID Namespace: virtualizing process IDs; process becoming PID 1 inside container while having standard PID on host.
  - `4.35.3` Mount Namespace (`mnt`): isolated filesystem hierarchy view; `pivot_root` and `chroot` mechanics.
  - `4.35.4` UTS Namespace: isolating hostnames and domain names without affecting host system.
- **Key Failure Modes & Edge Cases**: Running applications in containers with broken PID 1 setups, leading to un-reaped zombie processes and signal handling failures.
- **Verification & Mastery Check**: Create an isolated process manually using `unshare --pid --mount --uts --fork /bin/bash` and observe PID 1 mapping.
- **Project Application**: Core container architecture.', '{"solution.py": "# Phase 4 // Lesson 4.35: Linux Namespaces: Process, Mount, & UTS Isolation\n\ndef solve():\n    \"\"\"\n    Verification: Create an isolated process manually using `unshare --pid --mount --uts --fork /bin/bash` and observe\n    \"\"\"\n    pass\n"}'::jsonb, '{"lesson": "4.35", "subtopics_count": 4, "verification_criteria": "Create an isolated process manually using `unshare --pid --mount --uts --fork /bin/bash` and observe PID 1 mapping.", "subtopics": ["4.35.1 Demystifying containers: containers are standard Linux processes isolated via kernel namespaces and cgroups.", "4.35.2 PID Namespace: virtualizing process IDs; process becoming PID 1 inside container while having standard PID on host.", "4.35.3 Mount Namespace (`mnt`): isolated filesystem hierarchy view; `pivot_root` and `chroot` mechanics.", "4.35.4 UTS Namespace: isolating hostnames and domain names without affecting host system."]}'::jsonb, '["Explain how this implementation prevents: Running applications in containers with broken PID 1 setups, leading to un-reaped zombie processes and signal handling f", "How does Linux Namespaces: Process, Mount, & UTS Isolation scale under memory and concurrency constraints?", "Defend the architectural tradeoffs of this design in production."]'::jsonb)
